   $q$Rosa's savings account balance, in dollars, after $t$ years is $2000(1.06)^t$. Rewriting the factor as $\left(1.06^{\frac{1}{12}}\right)^{12t}$ shows the monthly growth. To the nearest hundredth of a percent, by what percent does the balance grow each month?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$6\\%$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"kept-annual-rate"},{"text":"$72\\%$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-instead-of-divided"},{"text":"$0.49\\%$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$0.5\\%$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"divided-annual-rate-by-12"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$0.49\%$$q$, $q$The monthly factor is $1.06^{\frac{1}{12}} \approx 1.0049$, so the balance grows about $0.49\%$ each month. Dividing 6% by 12 to get $0.5\%$ ignores compounding, which makes the true monthly rate slightly smaller.$q$, 'draft', null)
