   $q$A youth club rents a bus for 1200 dollars, and each rider also pays a 45 dollar museum ticket. With $n$ riders, the cost per rider is $\frac{1200 + 45n}{n}$, which equals $\frac{1200}{n} + 45$. What happens to the cost per rider as $n$ increases?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It increases, because $45n$ grows as $n$ grows.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"looked-only-at-numerator"},{"text":"It decreases toward 45 dollars but always stays above 45 dollars.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It stays at 1245 dollars for every rider.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-the-variable"},{"text":"It decreases toward 0 dollars.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-whole-expression-shrinks"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It decreases toward 45 dollars but always stays above 45 dollars.$q$, $q$In $\frac{1200}{n} + 45$, the bus share $\frac{1200}{n}$ shrinks toward 0 as $n$ grows, while the 45 dollar ticket stays, so the cost approaches 45 dollars from above. Saying it falls toward 0 forgets that the ticket cost does not get split.$q$, 'draft', null)
